    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0194" n="88" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg88"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 U.S.S.R./44: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-05-03T15:00:00-04:00">May 3, 1934—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>65. Your 71, May 2, 4 p.m. The answer to each of your three questions is in the
                    negative. I think you are correct in awaiting a move from Litvinoff. My belief
                    is that he is maneuvering to have negotiations transferred to Washington. Please
                    advise the moment Litvinoff approaches you, if at all.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
